Theorem IC-7: Convergence iteration limit soundness

  Preconditions:
    - IC-1 has decomposed the CallGraph into SCCs in forward
      topological order (callees before callers) per
      Annex E §AIMS IC-1 + sec-7 PL-1a
    - IC-2 (proven separately) seeds ParamContract bottoms;
      IC-3 (proven separately) joins ParamContract toward
      conservative across SCC fixpoint iterations; IC-4
      (proven separately) extracts ReturnContract per function;
      IC-5 (proven separately) derives EffectSummary per
      function; IC-6 (proven separately) is excluded from the
      Step 1 fixpoint per sec-5 IC-7 ("FipContract excluded
      (post-realization)")
    - IC-7 type signature (per Interprocedural.proof lines 49-54):
        compute_iteration_limit : FunctionId -> Int
      whose output is the upper bound on the number of Step 1
      interprocedural fixpoint iterations per SCC; on exceeded,
      the bound widens all contract values to most-conservative
      and emits a diagnostic per spec Annex E §AIMS IC-7
    - Spec statement (Annex E §AIMS IC-7): "Convergence:
      finite domain implies termination. Iteration limit SHALL
      be derived from domain heights: `param_count × 13 + 8 +
      6 + 4` (param=13, return=8, EffectSummary=6,
      ContextBehavior=4). FipContract excluded (post-
      realization). If exceeded, widen all to most conservative
      + emit diagnostic."
    - Shipped formula at compiler/ori_arc/src/aims/
      interprocedural/mod.rs lines 264-272:
        let total_height: u32 = scc_funcs
            .iter()
            .map(|f| {
                let param_height = f.params.len() as u32 * 17;
                let return_height = 8u32;
                let effect_height = 5u32;
                let context_height = 4u32;
                param_height + return_height + effect_height
                    + context_height
            })
            .sum();
      Spec-vs-shipped axis carve-out (per Annex E §AIMS
      "Target-only / partially-shipped subsystems" top-of-file
      + sec-5 IC-3 / IC-5 carve-outs):
        - Spec per-param-height = 13 (omits `may_escape` —
          derived from Locality per IC-3 spec).
        - Shipped per-param-height = 17. Per the inline comment
          at interprocedural/mod.rs lines 240-254: shipped
          ParamContract retains `may_escape` AND adds
          `return_alias: Option<ReturnAliasShape>` (height 2;
          chain `None < Some(Project) < Some(Direct)`) +
          `transfers_through_return: bool` (height 1) as a
          caller-side carrier. The shipped 17 = 13 (spec param
          height) + 1 (may_escape carve-out) + 1
          (transfers_through_return) + 2 (return_alias).
        - Spec effect-height = 6 (5 OR-joined fields incl.
          `may_read_inaccessible` + 1 one-shot
          `has_unbounded_stack`). Shipped effect-height = 5
          (`may_read_inaccessible` target-only per sec-5 IC-5
          carve-out; shipped struct lacks the field).
        - Spec return-height = 8 (4-dim ReturnContract:
          uniqueness 2 + preserves_freshness 1 + locality 4 +
          shape 1; sum = 8). Shipped return-height = 8 matches
          spec (the carve-out for `locality` / `shape`
          defaulting to CONSERVATIVE per sec-1.9 does NOT
          reduce chain height — defaulted dimensions still
          have positions in the join's domain).
        - Spec context-height = 4 (ContextBehavior 4 boolean
          fields: preserves_context + consumes_hole +
          requires_unique_context + may_resume_nonlinearly).
          Shipped context-height = 4 matches spec.
      Shipped formula bound is the soundness upper bound for
      the spec formula bound: 17 > 13, 5 < 6, but the shipped
      function-level bound `(17 × params + 8 + 5 + 4)` >=
      `(13 × params + 8 + 6 + 4)` for ALL `params >= 1` (the
      typical SCC member function). For `params = 0`, shipped
      `17 × 0 + 8 + 5 + 4 = 17` and spec `13 × 0 + 8 + 6 + 4
      = 18`; shipped < spec by 1. The off-by-one for zero-
      arity functions is documented in the inline comment at
      mod.rs lines 248-254 ("When may_escape is removed in a
      separate plan, this constant drops back to 16").
    - The IC-7 bound is enforced via `debug_assert!` at
      compiler/ori_arc/src/aims/interprocedural/
      mod.rs lines 274-278:
        debug_assert!(
            iterations <= total_height.saturating_add(1),
            "AIMS fixed-point exceeded convergence bound:
             {iterations} iterations for {total_height} height units"
        );
      The `saturating_add(1)` floor accommodates the
      acknowledgment iteration after the last height advance
      (per Tarski 1955: the fixpoint detection iteration runs
      one more pass after the last monotone increase to confirm
      stability).
    - Per L-5 (sec-1.8 lattice properties): every lattice
      dimension has finite height; product height = sum of
      dimension heights. The per-parameter height (17 shipped /
      13 spec) is the sum of ParamContract dimension heights;
      the return-height (8) is the sum of ReturnContract
      dimension heights; the effect-height (5 shipped / 6 spec)
      is the sum of EffectSummary OR-fold + AND-fold + one-shot
      heights; the context-height (4) is the sum of
      ContextBehavior 4-boolean-flag heights.
    - Per L-6 (sec-1.8): transfer functions SHALL be monotone;
      joins SHALL be monotone over each lattice dimension's
      partial order. Monotonicity is the foundational lattice
      property guaranteeing convergence per Tarski 1955 (least-
      fixpoint theorem on complete lattices).
    - Per Annex E §AIMS IC-2 ("each parameter initializes
      most optimistic") + IC-3 ("fixpoint promotes toward
      conservative"), the SCC fixpoint iteration starts at the
      BOTTOM of every lattice and monotonically advances toward
      conservative widening — never reverses. Each iteration
      either advances at least one lattice dimension by ≥1
      height unit OR converges (no change). The iteration count
      is bounded by the total advance possible across all
      dimensions of all functions in the SCC, which is the
      `total_height` formula.
    - Diagnostic on bound exceeded (spec sec-5 IC-7 "If
      exceeded, widen all to most conservative + emit
      diagnostic"): the shipped implementation uses
      `debug_assert!` (debug-only panic in debug builds; no-op
      in release builds). The widen-and-emit-diagnostic
      recovery path is target-only per the inline comment at
      mod.rs lines 274-278; current release builds silently
      assume the bound holds. The proof discharges the spec
      formula's soundness as an upper bound on iteration count;
      the widening / diagnostic recovery is enumerated drift
      tracked separately.

  Soundness property:
    Forall SCC `S = {f_1, f_2, ..., f_k}` with k functions and
    per-function parameter counts `[p_1, p_2, ..., p_k]`.
      Let `L := compute_iteration_limit(S)` be the emitted
      iteration bound:
        L_spec = sum_{i=1..k} (p_i × 13 + 8 + 6 + 4)
        L_shipped = sum_{i=1..k} (p_i × 17 + 8 + 5 + 4)
      Three conjuncts SHALL hold per spec Annex E §AIMS
      IC-7 + sec-1.8 L-5 + sec-1.8 L-6:

      (P1) Finite-height termination:
        The product lattice over the SCC's contract space has
        finite height equal to `L_spec` (per spec) or
        `L_shipped` (per shipped formula). Every SCC fixpoint
        iteration that does NOT converge SHALL advance at
        least one lattice dimension by ≥1 height unit; thus
        the iteration count is bounded above by the total
        height advance possible.

      (P2) Closed-form formula soundness:
        L_spec = sum_{i=1..k} (p_i × 13 + 8 + 6 + 4) is the
        sum of per-function product-lattice heights, where
        each function's contribution decomposes as:
          - per-parameter ParamContract height = 13 (sum of
            ParamContract dimension heights per spec sec-5
            IC-3 + carve-out: access(1) + consumption(3) +
            cardinality(2) + locality(4) + uniqueness(2) +
            may_share(1) = 13)
          - per-function ReturnContract height = 8 (sum of
            ReturnContract dimension heights per spec sec-5
            IC-4: uniqueness(2) + preserves_freshness(1) +
            locality(4) + shape(1) = 8)
          - per-function EffectSummary height = 6 (sum of
            EffectSummary OR-fold heights per spec sec-5
            IC-5: 5 OR-fields × 1 + 1 one-shot has_unbounded_stack
            × 1 = 6; alloc_only_on_slow_path AND-fold post-
            realization NOT included per IC-5 carve-out)
          - per-function ContextBehavior height = 4 (sum of
            ContextBehavior 4-boolean-flag heights per spec
            sec-5 PL-11: preserves_context(1) +
            consumes_hole(1) + requires_unique_context(1) +
            may_resume_nonlinearly(1) = 4)
        L_shipped applies the spec-vs-shipped carve-out:
          - per-parameter ParamContract height = 17 (13 spec
            + 1 may_escape + 1 transfers_through_return + 2
            return_alias per shipped struct)
          - per-function EffectSummary height = 5
            (may_read_inaccessible target-only)
          - per-function ReturnContract height = 8 (matches
            spec)
          - per-function ContextBehavior height = 4 (matches
            spec)

      (P3) Soundness on bound exceeded — widen + emit:
        IF iteration count `iterations` exceeds
        `total_height.saturating_add(1)`, the IC-7 contract
        SHALL widen every contract field to its most-
        conservative lattice value AND emit a diagnostic per
        spec sec-5 IC-7. The shipped `debug_assert!` panics in
        debug builds; the widen-and-emit-diagnostic recovery
        path is target-only and enumerated as drift per the
        Preconditions block above.

  Proof obligation:
    Three-part discharge corresponding to (P1)..(P3); finite-
    height termination (P1) grounds in L-5 + L-6 lattice
    properties + Tarski 1955 least-fixpoint theorem; closed-
    form formula soundness (P2) grounds in per-function
    decomposition of per-dimension heights per spec Annex E §AIMS IC-3 / IC-4 / IC-5 / PL-11; soundness on bound
    exceeded (P3) grounds in the spec sec-5 IC-7 recovery
    contract (widen + emit) with the shipped `debug_assert!`
    enforcement as a debug-only safety net.

    Part (P1) — Finite-height termination:

      Lattice-property foundation (per sec-1.8):
      - L-5: every dimension has finite height; product height
        = sum of dimension heights. The SCC's contract space
        is a product of per-function product lattices, where
        each per-function lattice is itself a product of
        ParamContract × ReturnContract × EffectSummary ×
        ContextBehavior dimension lattices. The total product
        has finite height equal to the sum of per-dimension
        heights across all functions and all parameters.
      - L-6: transfer functions and joins are monotone. The
        SCC fixpoint iteration applies IC-3 (ParamContract
        componentwise max-join), IC-4 (ReturnContract per-dim
        max-join + AND-monoid for preserves_freshness), IC-5
        (EffectSummary OR-fold), and PL-11 (ContextBehavior
        AND / OR join), all of which are monotone per their
        respective proofs (IC-3, IC-4, IC-5 proven separately;
        PL-11 proven in sec-02).

      Tarski 1955 (least-fixpoint theorem on complete
      lattices): a monotone function on a complete lattice has
      a least fixpoint, and iterative application of the
      function starting from the bottom converges to the LFP
      in at most `height(lattice)` iterations.

      Application to SCC fixpoint:
      - Start state: per IC-2, every parameter initializes to
        the most-optimistic state (BOTTOM of the lattice).
        Per IC-4 + IC-5 + PL-11, return / effect / context
        contracts initialize to the most-optimistic state
        (CONSERVATIVE for ReturnContract; OPTIMISTIC for
        EffectSummary + ContextBehavior).
      - Per-iteration transfer: each iteration applies the
        IC-3 / IC-4 / IC-5 / PL-11 join operators across all
        functions in the SCC, monotonically advancing contract
        fields toward conservative.
      - Termination criterion: an iteration converges iff NO
        contract field changes (no lattice dimension advanced
        in this iteration).
      - Bound: each non-converging iteration MUST advance at
        least one dimension by ≥1 height unit (else it would
        converge). The total possible advance is the
        `total_height` formula; thus the iteration count is
        bounded by `total_height + 1` (the +1 accommodates
        the acknowledgment iteration confirming stability).

      Per L-3 (idempotence of join): once a lattice dimension
      reaches its TOP, subsequent joins with any value yield
      the same TOP. The fixpoint detection iteration runs
      `total_height + 1` times in the worst case: `total_height`
      iterations each advancing at least one dimension by 1,
      plus 1 iteration confirming no further change. (P1)
      discharged.

    Part (P2) — Closed-form formula soundness:

      Per-parameter ParamContract height decomposition (spec
      sec-5 IC-3 + sec-1 lattice dimensions):

      | Dimension | Height | Chain |
      |---|---|---|
      | access | 1 | Borrowed < Owned |
      | consumption | 3 | Dead < Linear < Affine < Unrestricted |
      | cardinality | 2 | Absent < Once < Many |
      | locality | 4 | BlockLocal < FunctionLocal < ArgEscaping < HeapEscaping < Unknown |
      | uniqueness | 2 | Unique < MaybeShared < Shared |
      | may_share | 1 | false < true (OR-monoid) |
      | Total (spec) | **13** | (1+3+2+4+2+1 = 13) |

      Shipped ParamContract additionally carries:

      | Dimension | Height | Chain | Carve-out reason |
      |---|---|---|---|
      | may_escape | 1 | false < true | Shipped retains per top-of-file carve-out; spec derives from Locality |
      | transfers_through_return | 1 | false < true | BUG-04-090 caller-side gate (per mod.rs comment lines 240-246) |
      | return_alias | 2 | None < Some(Project) < Some(Direct) | BUG-04-090 caller-side carrier (per mod.rs comment lines 240-246) |
      | Total (shipped) | **17** | (13 + 1 + 1 + 2 = 17) |

      Per-function ReturnContract height (spec sec-5 IC-4 +
      sec-1.9 Return Provenance):

      | Dimension | Height | Chain |
      |---|---|---|
      | uniqueness | 2 | Unique < MaybeShared < Shared |
      | preserves_freshness | 1 | false < true (AND-monoid; identity true) |
      | locality | 4 | BlockLocal < FunctionLocal < ArgEscaping < HeapEscaping < Unknown |
      | shape | 1 | NonReusable < ReusableCtor / CollectionBuffer / ContextHole (flat) |
      | Total | **8** | (2+1+4+1 = 8) |

      Spec and shipped agree on ReturnContract height (8). The
      `locality` + `shape` carve-out in sec-1.9 (defaulting to
      CONSERVATIVE in shipped extractor) does NOT reduce chain
      height — the defaulted dimensions still occupy positions
      in the join's domain. The proof shape is preserved.

      Per-function EffectSummary height (spec sec-5 IC-5):

      | Dimension | Height | Note |
      |---|---|---|
      | may_allocate | 1 | OR-fold (false < true) |
      | may_deallocate | 1 | OR-fold (post-realization) |
      | may_share | 1 | OR-fold |
      | may_throw | 1 | OR-fold |
      | has_unbounded_stack | 1 | One-shot from SCC + tail-position |
      | may_read_inaccessible | 1 | OR-fold (target-only) |
      | Total (spec) | **6** | (5 OR-fields + 1 one-shot = 6) |
      | Total (shipped) | **5** | (4 OR-fields + 1 one-shot = 5; may_read_inaccessible carve-out) |

      The `alloc_only_on_slow_path` AND-fold is POST-
      REALIZATION + intraprocedural-only per sec-5 IC-5; it
      does NOT contribute to the Step 1 fixpoint iteration
      bound. The IC-5 proof's height calculation matches:
      5 (OR-fields) + 1 (one-shot has_unbounded_stack) = 6
      per spec; minus may_read_inaccessible = 5 per shipped.

      Per-function ContextBehavior height (spec sec-5 PL-11):

      | Dimension | Height | Join |
      |---|---|---|
      | preserves_context | 1 | AND |
      | consumes_hole | 1 | AND |
      | requires_unique_context | 1 | OR |
      | may_resume_nonlinearly | 1 | OR |
      | Total | **4** | (4 boolean fields × 1 = 4) |

      Spec and shipped agree on ContextBehavior height (4).

      Per-function total (sum of per-function components):
        spec = p × 13 + 8 + 6 + 4
        shipped = p × 17 + 8 + 5 + 4

      SCC total (sum over all functions in SCC):
        L_spec = sum_{i=1..k} (p_i × 13 + 8 + 6 + 4)
        L_shipped = sum_{i=1..k} (p_i × 17 + 8 + 5 + 4)

      The shipped formula at mod.rs lines 264-272 implements
      L_shipped verbatim. Each per-function contribution is
      summed across the SCC; the cumulative bound is enforced
      via `debug_assert!` at mod.rs lines 274-278. (P2)
      discharged.

    Part (P3) — Soundness on bound exceeded:

      Spec recovery contract (sec-5 IC-7):
      "If exceeded, widen all to most conservative + emit
      diagnostic."

      Soundness rationale:
      The widen-on-bound-exceeded recovery is the safety net
      ensuring soundness even when the closed-form bound
      under-estimates the true iteration count (e.g., due to a
      monotonicity bug in transfer functions or a lattice-
      property regression). By widening all contract fields to
      their most-conservative value (CONSERVATIVE for
      ParamContract per IC-3, CONSERVATIVE for ReturnContract
      per IC-4, ALL for EffectSummary per IC-5, most-pessimistic
      for ContextBehavior per PL-11), the recovery path
      guarantees the emitted contracts are sound — they
      over-approximate observed demands across all call sites,
      even at the cost of precision.

      Per L-6 (monotonicity), widening to the most-conservative
      value is the lattice TOP, which is the soundness floor
      for downstream consumers (Step 5 emission, Step 5a
      verification, RL-29 + RL-30 LLVM fact-export gates). A
      widened contract may lose optimization opportunities but
      cannot produce silent miscompilation.

      Per the diagnostic emission requirement, the user (or CI
      pipeline) is notified of the convergence failure so they
      can file a bug (per CLAUDE.md sec-Bug Handling: discovery
      IS the assignment). The diagnostic surfaces the
      convergence anomaly for investigation, preventing the
      bug from silently degrading optimization quality.

      Shipped implementation (mod.rs lines 274-278):
        debug_assert!(
            iterations <= total_height.saturating_add(1),
            "AIMS fixed-point exceeded convergence bound:
             {iterations} iterations for {total_height} height units"
        );

      The `debug_assert!` panics in debug builds with a
      diagnostic message; in release builds, the assertion is
      compiled out (per Rust's debug-only `debug_assert!`
      semantics). The widen-and-emit-diagnostic recovery path
      is NOT implemented in shipped — the release build
      silently assumes the bound holds.

      Spec-vs-shipped carve-out: the widen-and-emit recovery is
      enumerated drift per the Preconditions block. The proof
      discharges the spec formula's soundness as an upper bound
      on iteration count; the recovery path is tracked
      separately. When wired, the recovery applies CONSERVATIVE
      values across all dimensions per the spec rule, and the
      diagnostic surfaces via the ori_diagnostic infrastructure
      (per CLAUDE.md sec-Diagnostics).

      Per CLAUDE.md sec-AIMS Invariant 4 ("the enabled surface
      must be end-to-end verified"), the missing recovery path
      is a known target-only gap. The `debug_assert!` provides
      end-to-end verification in debug builds; release builds
      currently rely on the formula being a sound upper bound
      (which the proof above establishes via L-5 + L-6 +
      Tarski 1955). (P3) discharged for the spec contract;
      shipped recovery enumerated.

      Negative-direction witness — under-counting per-parameter
      height rejected:

      Suppose hypothetical implementer sets per-parameter-
      height = 6 (sum of access + consumption + cardinality +
      uniqueness, omitting locality + may_share):

        let param_height = f.params.len() as u32 * 6; // BANNED

      Then the iteration bound would under-estimate the
      ParamContract advance possible. A function with a
      parameter undergoing locality advance from BlockLocal
      → FunctionLocal → ArgEscaping → HeapEscaping → Unknown
      (4 transitions) PLUS uniqueness advance from Unique →
      MaybeShared → Shared (2 transitions) PLUS access
      advance from Borrowed → Owned (1 transition) would
      exceed the under-estimated bound (6 < 13) on a single
      parameter undergoing all transitions in sequence.

      The `debug_assert!` would fire in debug builds, surfacing
      the convergence anomaly. In release builds (where the
      assertion is compiled out), the SCC fixpoint iteration
      would continue past the under-estimated bound but
      eventually converge (per L-5 + L-6 + Tarski 1955),
      because the true height bound is the SPEC formula. The
      under-counted bound is unsound as a CONVERGENCE GUARANTEE
      but does not produce wrong output — only loss of
      diagnostic precision.

      Per CLAUDE.md sec-AIMS Invariant 3 ("no pass relies on
      stale summaries"), the diagnostic gap is a correctness
      regression even though the converged contracts remain
      sound. The shipped formula at mod.rs lines 264-272 uses
      the SHIPPED constants (17 / 8 / 5 / 4), which over-
      estimate the spec bound for non-zero arities and match
      spec for ContextBehavior. The over-estimate is sound
      (covers spec bound); under-estimate is unsound
      (diagnostic gap). The shipped formula is therefore the
      soundness upper bound for the spec formula.

    Coherence side-conditions for (P1)..(P3):

      - L-5 + L-6 lemmas (sec-1.8) provide the foundational
        lattice properties: finite height + monotone joins +
        monotone transfer functions. Tarski 1955 (least-
        fixpoint theorem on complete lattices) provides the
        convergence guarantee: a monotone function on a
        complete lattice has a least fixpoint, reachable in
        at most `height(lattice)` iterations.

      - The SCC fixpoint iteration applies IC-3 + IC-4 + IC-5
        + PL-11 joins across all functions in the SCC. Per
        IC-3 (ParamContract componentwise max-join with
        OR-monoid for may_share), IC-4 (ReturnContract per-
        dim max-join + AND-monoid for preserves_freshness),
        IC-5 (EffectSummary OR-fold + AND-fold + one-shot
        SCC-derived), and PL-11 (ContextBehavior AND-monoid +
        OR-monoid), every join is monotone per L-6. The
        per-function transfer function (per IC-2 seeding +
        IC-3 / IC-4 / IC-5 / PL-11 joins) is therefore
        monotone over the product lattice, and Tarski's
        theorem applies.

      - The closed-form formula L_spec / L_shipped is the
        per-dimension height decomposition of the product
        lattice, summed across all functions in the SCC. Per
        L-5, the product-lattice height is the sum of
        dimension heights; the formula is therefore exact for
        the spec lattice (L_spec) and an upper bound for the
        shipped lattice (L_shipped > L_spec for params >= 1).

      - The `saturating_add(1)` floor at mod.rs line 275
        accommodates the acknowledgment iteration confirming
        stability (per Tarski's iterative computation: the
        fixpoint is reached when one full iteration produces
        no change; this acknowledgment iteration is NOT a
        height advance but is REQUIRED for termination
        detection). Without the +1, the bound would
        under-count by exactly one iteration, causing
        spurious debug-assertion failures on legitimate
        convergence runs.

      - The diagnostic emission requirement (P3) is the
        soundness safety net for the case where the
        closed-form bound under-estimates true convergence
        cost (e.g., due to a monotonicity bug in transfer
        functions). Widening to CONSERVATIVE values per IC-3 /
        IC-4 / IC-5 / PL-11 lattice TOPs ensures the emitted
        contracts remain sound even under convergence failure.

    Convergence side-condition — recursive:

      IC-7 is the convergence-bound rule for the Step 1 fixpoint
      loop itself. The proof's correctness IS the convergence
      guarantee — the bound formula sums per-dimension heights
      across all functions in the SCC, and L-5 + L-6 + Tarski
      1955 establish that the SCC fixpoint converges within
      `total_height + 1` iterations.

      Exclusion of FipContract (IC-6) per sec-5 IC-7:
      FipContract is explicitly excluded from the iteration
      bound formula because its authoritative value is computed
      at Step 5a (POST-REALIZATION) per sec-5 IC-6 + sec-7
      PL-1a. The Step 1 fixpoint does not iterate on FipContract
      values; the Step 1 provisional Never seed is overwritten
      by Step 5a's authoritative computation. Per the IC-6
      proof (proven separately), the timing separation is
      load-bearing for AIMS pipeline acyclicity.

    Coverage gate — corpus enumeration:

      The shipped iteration-bound enforcement at mod.rs lines
      264-278 is exercised by the interprocedural test surface
      in compiler/ori_arc/src/aims/
      interprocedural/tests.rs + adjacent test modules. Each
      row below pins one or more (P1)..(P3) conjuncts:

        Row C1: zero_arity_single_function_converges_within_bound —
                function with 0 parameters; iteration bound =
                17 (shipped) / 18 (spec); convergence within
                bound; (P1) + (P2) zero-arity base case pin
        Row C2: single_arity_single_function_converges_within_bound —
                function with 1 parameter; iteration bound =
                17 + 8 + 5 + 4 = 34 (shipped); convergence
                within bound; (P2) per-parameter height pin
        Row C3: multi_arity_single_function_converges_within_bound —
                function with 5 parameters; iteration bound =
                5 × 17 + 8 + 5 + 4 = 102 (shipped); convergence
                within bound; (P2) multi-parameter linearity pin
        Row C4: paramcontract_locality_advances_consume_height —
                function whose parameter undergoes BlockLocal
                → FunctionLocal → ArgEscaping → HeapEscaping
                → Unknown advance across 4 SCC iterations;
                4 height units consumed; (P1) Locality
                dimension height pin
        Row C5: paramcontract_uniqueness_advances_consume_height —
                function whose parameter undergoes Unique →
                MaybeShared → Shared advance across 2 SCC
                iterations; 2 height units consumed; (P1)
                Uniqueness dimension height pin
        Row C6: paramcontract_consumption_advances_consume_height —
                function whose parameter undergoes Dead →
                Linear → Affine → Unrestricted advance across
                3 SCC iterations; 3 height units consumed;
                (P1) Consumption dimension height pin
        Row C7: returncontract_locality_advances_consume_height —
                function whose return value undergoes
                BlockLocal → Unknown advance; 4 height units
                consumed; (P1) ReturnContract Locality pin
        Row C8: effectsummary_or_field_advances_consume_height —
                function whose may_allocate / may_deallocate /
                may_share / may_throw OR-fields all advance
                from false → true; 4 height units consumed;
                (P1) EffectSummary OR-fold height pin
        Row C9: contextbehavior_and_field_advances_consume_height —
                TRMC function whose preserves_context /
                consumes_hole AND-fields advance from true →
                false; 2 height units consumed; (P1)
                ContextBehavior AND-fold height pin
        Row C10: scc_mutual_recursion_converges_within_bound —
                 mutual-recursion SCC where A calls B and
                 vice versa; per-function bound sums; (P1) +
                 (P2) SCC topological ordering pin
        Row C11: large_scc_converges_within_bound —
                 SCC with 10 functions × 5 params each;
                 iteration bound = 10 × (5 × 17 + 8 + 5 + 4)
                 = 1020 (shipped); convergence within bound;
                 (P2) large-SCC scalability pin
        Row C12: debug_assert_fires_on_bound_exceeded —
                 test that artificially inflates iteration
                 count past the bound; `debug_assert!`
                 panics in debug build; (P3) safety-net pin
        Row C13: saturating_add_handles_overflow —
                 edge case: extremely large SCC where
                 `total_height + 1` would overflow u32;
                 `saturating_add(1)` caps at u32::MAX; (P3)
                 overflow-safety pin
        Row C14: zero_iteration_count_within_bound —
                 SCC that converges in 0 iterations (single-
                 function SCC with no contract changes from
                 initial seeds); bound holds trivially;
                 (P1) base-case pin
        Row C15: fipcontract_excluded_from_iteration_count —
                 SCC whose Step 1 fixpoint converges, and
                 FipContract values are NOT counted toward
                 the iteration bound (per sec-5 IC-7
                 exclusion); (P2) FipContract-exclusion pin
        Row C16: iteration_acknowledgment_accommodated_by_plus_1 —
                 SCC that requires exactly `total_height`
                 advances + 1 acknowledgment iteration to
                 detect stability; `saturating_add(1)`
                 accommodates the acknowledgment without
                 firing the debug assertion; (P1) + (P3)
                 acknowledgment-iteration pin
        Row C17: tarski_lfp_convergence_pin —
                 monotone SCC fixpoint reaches LFP within
                 `total_height + 1` iterations per Tarski
                 1955; verified via instrumented iteration
                 count; (P1) Tarski theorem application pin

      Coverage axes:
        Closed-form formula correctness (P2):
          Rows C1-C3 + C11 pin the per-parameter linearity +
          per-function constants + SCC summation; Rows C4-C9
          pin per-dimension height contributions
        Finite-height termination (P1):
          Rows C4-C9 directly pin each dimension's height
          consumption; Rows C10 + C11 pin SCC-scope termination;
          Rows C14 + C17 pin Tarski LFP base case + general case
        Safety-net behavior (P3):
          Row C12 pins the `debug_assert!` panic on bound
          exceeded; Row C13 pins overflow safety
        Acknowledgment iteration:
          Row C16 pins the `saturating_add(1)` floor accommodating
          the stability-detection acknowledgment iteration
        FipContract exclusion:
          Row C15 pins the IC-7 exclusion of FipContract per
          sec-5 IC-7

    Negative-direction witness — replacing sum with product
    rejected:

      Take Row C10 (scc_mutual_recursion_converges_within_bound).
      Suppose hypothetical implementer replaces the sum-fold
      with a product-fold:

        let total_height: u32 = scc_funcs
            .iter()
            .map(|f| f.params.len() as u32 * 17 + 8 + 5 + 4)
            .product(); // BANNED — product instead of sum

      Then the iteration bound would grow combinatorially with
      SCC size — a 10-function SCC where each function has
      bound 34 would have product bound 34^10 approx 2.06 times 10^15,
      vastly over-counting the true height. The
      `debug_assert!` would still hold (over-counting is sound
      for the bound), but the formula would be wrong as a
      LATTICE HEIGHT computation per L-5 (which mandates SUM
      of dimension heights, not product).

      Per L-5: "every dimension SHALL have finite height;
      product height = sum of dimension heights". The lattice
      product's height is the SUM of component heights, not
      the product — the formula at mod.rs lines 264-272 correctly
      uses `.sum()`. Replacing with `.product()` would be a
      lattice-theory misunderstanding even though the resulting
      bound is sound (over-approximates the true bound).

    Negative-direction witness — removing acknowledgment +1
    rejected:

      Take Row C16 (iteration_acknowledgment_accommodated_by_
      plus_1). Suppose hypothetical implementer removes the
      `saturating_add(1)`:

        debug_assert!(
            iterations <= total_height, // BANNED — no +1
            "..."
        );

      Then SCCs that converge in exactly `total_height + 1`
      iterations (`total_height` height advances + 1
      acknowledgment iteration) would spuriously fire the
      debug assertion, even though the convergence is sound.
      Per Tarski 1955: the iterative computation of the LFP
      requires one "confirmation pass" after the last height
      advance to detect that no further change occurs. The
      confirmation pass is NOT a height advance but IS a
      required iteration in the worst-case bound.

      Per L-3 (idempotence of join), the confirmation pass's
      joins yield the same values as the prior iteration
      (acknowledgment of stability). The `saturating_add(1)`
      floor accommodates this without inflating the bound by
      more than 1 iteration. (P1) discharged.

    IC-7 dependencies — supporting role within AIMS contract
    layer:

      IC-7 consumes the per-function parameter counts (from
      the IR) + per-dimension height constants (from sec-1.8
      L-5 + per-rule height tables at IC-3 / IC-4 / IC-5 /
      PL-11). It produces a single u32 iteration bound per
      SCC. The dependency chain at the proof-obligation level
      is acyclic:

        IC-1 --> SCC decomposition feeds the per-SCC
                   iteration bound computation
        L-5 (sec-1.8 finite-height lemma) --> IC-7 (closed-
                                                form formula
                                                soundness)
        L-6 (sec-1.8 monotonicity lemma) --> IC-7 (Tarski
                                                applicability)
        IC-3 (sec-5 ParamContract heights) --> IC-7 (per-
                                                  parameter
                                                  height = 13
                                                  spec / 17
                                                  shipped)
        IC-4 (sec-5 ReturnContract heights) --> IC-7 (per-
                                                   function
                                                   return
                                                   height = 8)
        IC-5 (sec-5 EffectSummary heights) --> IC-7 (per-
                                                  function
                                                  effect
                                                  height = 6
                                                  spec / 5
                                                  shipped)
        PL-11 (sec-5 ContextBehavior heights) --> IC-7 (per-
                                                     function
                                                     context
                                                     height = 4)
        IC-7 --> Step 1 fixpoint loop (consumes bound to
                                         detect convergence
                                         failure)
        IC-7 --> Diagnostic emission (target-only, sec-5
                                        IC-7 recovery contract)
        IC-7 --> FipContract EXCLUDED (sec-5 IC-7 explicit
                                         carve-out per sec-5
                                         IC-6 post-realization
                                         timing)

      IC-7 is the convergence-bound enforcement rule within
      the IC layer; L-5 + L-6 + per-rule height tables are
      proven upstream in sec-02 + per-rule IC proofs.

    Algorithm-citation declaration:
      algorithm: per-SCC closed-form iteration bound
                           = sum over functions of (params ×
                           per-param-height + return-height +
                           effect-height + context-height);
                           FipContract excluded (post-
                           realization timing per sec-5 IC-6 +
                           PL-1a); on bound exceeded, widen
                           all contracts to most-conservative
                           lattice TOP + emit diagnostic per
                           spec sec-5 IC-7 recovery contract
      published basis: Tarski, A., "A lattice-theoretical
                           fixpoint theorem and its
                           applications", Pacific Journal of
                           Mathematics, vol. 5, no. 2,
                           pp. 285-309, 1955 (least-fixpoint
                           theorem on complete lattices —
                           monotone function on complete
                           lattice has LFP, reachable in at
                           most height(lattice) iterations);
                           Birkhoff, G., "Lattice Theory",
                           3rd ed., American Mathematical
                           Society Colloquium Publications,
                           vol. XXV, 1967, §I.5 (totally-
                           ordered chains under max), §V
                           (product lattices — height equals
                           sum of component heights); Cousot
                           & Cousot, "Abstract Interpretation:
                           A Unified Lattice Model for Static
                           Analysis of Programs by
                           Construction or Approximation of
                           Fixpoints", POPL 1977
                           (interprocedural summary
                           computation via SCC-topological
                           monotone iteration with widening +
                           closed-form iteration bounds for
                           termination guarantee)
      complexity: O(scc_size) per `total_height`
                           computation (single sum-fold over
                           SCC functions); O(1) per per-
                           function contribution (constant-
                           time arithmetic on parameter count);
                           O(scc_size × iteration_count) per
                           Step 1 fixpoint loop, where
                           iteration_count <=
                           total_height.saturating_add(1)
      determinism: deterministic; closed-form formula
                           is a pure total function of the
                           per-function parameter counts;
                           per-dimension height constants are
                           compile-time constants; SCC
                           topological order is consistent
                           across compilation runs per IC-1;
                           debug-assertion-firing is
                           deterministic in debug builds
      reference impl: iteration bound formula at
                           compiler/ori_arc/src/
                           aims/interprocedural/mod.rs lines
                           264-272; `debug_assert!`
                           enforcement at lines 274-278;
                           inline comment documenting the
                           17-vs-13 spec-vs-shipped axis at
                           lines 240-254
      cross-language Lean 4
      prior art: `lean4/src/Lean/Compiler/IR/Borrow.lean`
                           uses similar SCC-based fixpoint
                           with closed-form iteration bound
                           derived from per-dimension height
                           sums; Koka Perceus
                           (`koka/src/Core/Borrowed.hs`)
                           applies similar bounded-iteration
                           convergence detection; Swift
                           SILOptimizer's `ARCSequenceOpt`
                           uses similar product-lattice
                           height computation for ARC
                           dataflow convergence; FP² paper's
                           Theorem 4 formalizes the closed-
                           form iteration bound for
                           interprocedural FBIP analysis that
                           AIMS implements; arc.md
                           §Reference Repos cites Lean 4 +
                           Swift ARC + Koka Perceus as the
                           prior art set

